      "heading": "UK temperature limits, plainly",
      "html": "<p>The Food Standards Agency says chilled food must be kept at 8C or below, a legal requirement in England, Wales and Northern Ireland and a recommendation in Scotland, and that fridges should be set to 5C or below. Its guidance says hot food must be kept at or above 63C if it is not served straight away. For cooking, its standard advice is a core of 70C for two minutes, or an equal time and temperature such as 75C for 30 seconds. These cooking figures are advice, not stated as law. Cooked food not served hot should be cooled as fast as possible and chilled.</p><p>Your log checks against these. An app does not keep the fridge cold. It proves the check happened and flags a drift. Check current Food Standards Agency guidance for exact figures, and Food Standards Scotland if you are in Scotland. This is not legal advice.</p>"
